---
Scope: styles.css BK3 block (appended `:where(button, [role="button"], a[href],
summary, [tabindex="0"]):focus-visible { outline: 2px solid rgb(var(--accent));
outline-offset: 2px; }`). `git diff master` on this file also contains BK1's
input/select/textarea box-shadow rule (already accepted, not re-audited here
except as context for point 4). Foreign/attribute-only, never FAILed here:
BK2's major-expenses.html + render_major_expenses_test.go changes (accepted
separately).

Harness (per instructions, real render + real built CSS, not hand-built HTML):
worktree server on :8214 (`scripts/whatif-verify.sh start 8214` in
/home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2/.claude/worktrees/backlog-a11y-2, branch
feat/backlog-a11y-2, base 176383f); master baseline on :8224 via a `.git`-
stripped `cp -a` of /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2 (HEAD 176383f) into scratch
`master-copy/`, same start script. Both torn down with `stop` at the end of
this audit. Playwright (chromium-1243, --no-sandbox) scripts in scratch
`node/` (lib.js, point1.js, pixelcheck.js/analyze2.py, point2.js, point3.js,
point4.js, axecheck.js) drive real navigation + REAL keyboard Tab (see
methodology note below), never scripted CSS assumptions.

Real-Tab methodology: reload page (fresh focus-visible modality) → optional
`#theme-toggle` / `#theme-toggle-mobile` click + `page.reload()` for dark →
PROGRAMMATICALLY focus (`el.focus()`, bypassing Playwright's mouse-click
actionability, which false-negatives on elements laid out inside a CLOSED
native `<details>` — Chromium keeps a real, nonzero `getBoundingClientRect()`
for that content but silently refuses `.focus()` on it; a mouse-click
approach hangs against `#major-expenses-add-form`'s always-closed accordion,
a pre-existing, out-of-scope trait) the element immediately preceding target
in tab order → one real `page.keyboard.press('Tab')` → assert
`document.activeElement` matches target. Because Chromium's `:focus-visible`
heuristic keys off the modality of the operation that produced the CURRENT
focus (the Tab keydown), the resulting ring on the TARGET is indistinguishable
from a user tabbing there by hand. `.major-expense-row-toggle` and the
Restore/Discard buttons required opening `#major-expenses-definitions-panel`
and `#major-expenses-deleted-panel` (both closed `<details>` by default) via
`el.open = true` before the Tab walk.

=== Point 1: unstyled controls now ringed — computed style + contrast ===

Command: `node point1.js` (focus via real Tab, computed outline + naive
ancestor-backgroundColor contrast), corroborated pixel-for-pixel by
`node pixelcheck.js` + `python3 analyze2.py` (screenshots the focused
control, samples the actual rendered outline pixel and background pixel).

- major-expense-row-toggle (/major-expenses): outline solid 2px
  rgb(79,70,229) light / rgb(165,180,252) dark, offset 2px. Card bg
  white/gray-800. Pixel-measured ratio: 6.29:1 light, 7.61:1 dark. PASS.
- restore-btn / discard-btn (/major-expenses, deleted panel open): same
  ring, same card. Pixel-measured ratio: 6.29:1 light, 7.61:1 dark
  (confirmed by dumping the raw pixel row — a same-crop stray pixel from
  the adjacent button's own colored border was a detection-script
  artifact, not a rendering defect; the accent ring itself is present and
  correctly colored in every sample). PASS.
- summary (Period details, /dashboard): 6.29:1 light, 7.61:1 dark. PASS.
- transfers-scroll-region (div[tabindex=0][role=region], /transfers):
  6.29:1 light, 7.61:1 dark. PASS.
- **nav-link (a[href="/explorer..."], /dashboard, desktop 1300) — FAIL.**
  `getComputedStyle().backgroundColor` walk falsely reports the PAGE
  background because the nav bar's fill is a `background-image` gradient
  (`bg-gradient-to-r from-accent-strong to-accent-strong
  dark:from-gray-800 dark:to-gray-900`), not `background-color`. Real
  pixel sample (`node navshot2.js` → screenshot → PIL) of the actual
  rendered nav bar behind the ring:
    - LIGHT: ring pixel = rgb(79,70,229), header pixel = rgb(79,70,229).
      **Contrast ratio 1.00:1 — the ring is IDENTICAL to its background,
      completely invisible.** Root cause: `--accent: 79 70 229` and
      `--accent-strong: 79 70 229` are defined as the SAME value in light
      mode (styles.css lines 35/37), and the nav's light-mode fill is
      `accent-strong` while BK3's fallback ring uses `--accent`.
    - DARK: ring rgb(165,180,252) vs header rgb(36,33,31) (gray-800/900
      gradient, not accent-strong in dark mode) = 8.03:1. Fine.
  **Master comparison (both ends measured, per the acceptance text's own
  tie-break):** `node navshot2.js` against :8224 shows master's
  browser-default `outline:auto` painting a genuine adaptive double ring
  (white outer + black inner) at this exact location:
    - LIGHT: white-vs-header 6.29:1, black-vs-header 3.03:1 — both clear
      the 3:1 bar, clearly visible in the screenshot.
    - DARK: white-vs-header 16.14:1.
  Master's ring was strictly better here in light mode (6.29:1/3.03:1 vs
  BK3's 1.00:1) — this is a REGRESSION, not a wash, so per the brief's own
  rule ("that is a FAIL only if master's default ring was better there —
  measure both") this is a **FAIL**.
- **mobile-nav-toggle (#mobile-nav-toggle, /dashboard, mobile 390) — FAIL,
  same root cause.** Same `<nav>` bar, same light-mode accent-strong fill.
  Pixel row dump (`analyze2.py` / raw dump) at 390 viewport: LIGHT — 20+
  consecutive pixels all rgb(79,70,229), no ring stripe detectable at all
  (ratio ~1.00:1, effectively invisible); DARK — clean ring at
  rgb(165,180,252) vs rgb(~30,27,25) header, high contrast, fine.

Screenshots backing these two: scratch node/nav-worktree-light.png (ring
invisible), node/nav-master-light.png (double ring visible),
node/px-mobile-nav-toggle-light.png (no visible stripe).

=== Point 2: styled controls unchanged vs master ===

Command: `node point2.js` — focus each via real Tab on :8214 and :8224,
diff the computed outline+box-shadow object.
- #import-csv-btn (focus:outline-none + focus-visible:ring-2
  focus-visible:ring-accent): SAME in both themes (outline suppressed,
  box-shadow ring only) — PASS.
- [data-wf-tab="overview"] (own outline rule): SAME both themes — PASS.
- [data-ins-tab="trends"]: SAME both themes — PASS.
- **[data-kpi-detail="income"] (KPI tile, `focus-visible:ring-2
  focus-visible:ring-accent rounded` — NO `focus:outline-none`) — FAIL,
  not identical.** Master: `outlineStyle: auto, outlineWidth: 1px,
  outlineOffset: 0px, outlineColor: rgb(16,16,16)` (browser default) PLUS
  the same accent box-shadow ring. Worktree: BK3's `:where()` rule now
  also wins the outline slot here (this button never set `outline:none`,
  so `:where()`'s author-stylesheet vs UA-stylesheet cascade priority
  lets it override the UA default regardless of the 0-specificity design)
  → `outlineStyle: solid, outlineWidth: 2px, outlineOffset: 2px,
  outlineColor: rgb(79,70,229)` PLUS the unchanged box-shadow ring.
  Screenshots (node/kpi-master.png vs node/kpi-worktree.png) show master's
  thin, tight double-ring becomes a visibly thicker doubled accent ring on
  the worktree (outer 2px-offset outline + inner box-shadow ring, same
  hue). Not a contrast/color-only violation (still ≥6:1, single hue,
  clearly visible) and not a hidden-focus defect, but it violates this
  task's own explicit acceptance text ("computed outline ... identical to
  master's ... no double indicator") — the `:where()` design's stated
  guarantee ("only controls with no focus utility get the ring") does not
  hold for controls that have a ring utility but no outline utility, which
  is exactly this KPI-tile pattern (also present on the other
  `data-kpi-detail` buttons, all sharing this class list per the dashboard
  template).

